      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i guess my concern is more about the unexpected stuff. sometimes you have those weird, little issues that pop up out of nowhere even with all those gates in place—like something small and annoying that just bypasses everything perfectly. do you have a way to categorize or describe those kinds of random, pesky errors that sneak through? like how we might describe things that feel like they're just working against us?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "These elusive issues that bypass standard gates are often categorized by automation teams as \"Surface Issues\" or \"False Negatives,\" where a flaw exists but the pre-release checks failed to detect it due to incomplete test coverage or edge-case gaps. They are frequently described as a \"Trojan Horse\" release, where the functionality appears to deploy smoothly on all acceptance criteria, only to fail catastrophically at the moment of real-world usage. To defend against this, many organizations employ \"Chaos Engineering\" to proactively inject failures into staging environments, ensuring the pipeline is resilient enough to surface these hidden bugs before they ever reach production. This shifts the mindset from simply catching errors to stress-testing the system's ability to handle the unexpected exactly where it slips through the cracks.",
      "source": "target",
